# Topic: work that grows with a library call's input
#
# What it does: counts, for a script's work, what the library and engine calls it makes do by size, in slots of the work counters'
# hostops that no opcode reaches (lua.h LUA_WORK_SCANNED and below): table elements table.find compared; elements table.insert and
# table.remove at a position moved, table.move copied, table.unpack / table.pack pushed and table.clear cleared; bytes of every string
# the script's work made or looked up (luaS_newlstr, luaS_buffinish); bytes a plain search read; the pattern matcher's steps, a
# character class's test apart; bytes string.upper / lower converted, a utf8 function decoded, characters string.char and codepoints
# utf8.char made, values string.byte pushed; buffers made and the bytes a buffer function zeroed, filled, copied or wrote (a large
# new buffer's, and a copy's, fill's or string write's over 64 KB, again); the bytes of every object and table part the script's
# work allocated, its instructions' and its library and engine calls' alike, the objects
# those calls made, every object again for the collector, which sweeps each, the allocations too large for the VM's own pages, and the
# keys a table's growth put in a hash part. A host marks its own C functions (lua_sethostcfunction): what they allocate is part of
# the operation the host prices, so these counts leave it out. A profile books these slots at every step, with the cold-call slots
# it missed before.
#
# Why upstream lacks it: an emulator's work clock charges a script's work as a Roblox client spends it (src/cost.rs); a flat price per
# call reads a 100,000-element table.find or a 1 MB gsub as a short one. Roblox's VM runs on the real clock and needs no counts.

